WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.083
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:08.758
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.058 --> 00:00:15.600
Unit thirteen: Oral History. Today you will speculate about what oral sources may reveal with might and may.

4
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.668
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.947
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:24.993
Oral history.

7
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:30.038
Oral history means history gathered from people's spoken memories.

8
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.008
Oral history captures voices missing from archives.

9
00:00:34.617 --> 00:00:37.977
Your turn. Say: Oral history.

10
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:42.055
Oral history.

11
00:00:42.817 --> 00:00:43.771
Testimony.

12
00:00:44.783 --> 00:00:47.909
Testimony means a personal account given as evidence.

13
00:00:48.517 --> 00:00:51.399
Her testimony described life in the work camps.

14
00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:54.612
Your turn. Say: Testimony.

15
00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:58.433
Testimony.

16
00:00:59.183 --> 00:01:00.161
Interview.

17
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.566
Interview means a formal conversation to gather information.

18
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.684
Each interview lasted about two hours.

19
00:01:08.283 --> 00:01:10.843
Your turn. Say: Interview.

20
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:14.585
Interview.

21
00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:16.711
Firsthand account.

22
00:01:17.717 --> 00:01:21.667
Firsthand account means a description from someone who experienced events.

23
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:24.991
A firsthand account adds emotional detail.

24
00:01:25.583 --> 00:01:28.758
Your turn. Say: Firsthand account.

25
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:32.973
Firsthand account.

26
00:01:33.717 --> 00:01:34.660
Memory.

27
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:39.484
Memory means the ability to remember, or something remembered.

28
00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:42.129
Memory can change over decades.

29
00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:45.032
Your turn. Say: Memory.

30
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:48.717
Memory.

31
00:01:49.483 --> 00:01:50.426
Archive.

32
00:01:51.417 --> 00:01:54.194
Archive means a collection where records are kept.

33
00:01:54.783 --> 00:01:57.282
The recordings were stored in a digital archive.

34
00:01:57.883 --> 00:01:59.990
Your turn. Say: Archive.

35
00:02:02.833 --> 00:02:03.625
Archive.

36
00:02:04.383 --> 00:02:05.222
Narrator.

37
00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:08.808
Narrator means the person telling their story.

38
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:12.612
The narrator paused before describing the evacuation.

39
00:02:13.217 --> 00:02:15.416
Your turn. Say: Narrator.

40
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:19.152
Narrator.

41
00:02:19.917 --> 00:02:21.173
Authenticity.

42
00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:25.646
Authenticity means the quality of being genuine and reliable.

43
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.515
Historians assess the authenticity of each account.

44
00:02:30.117 --> 00:02:33.431
Your turn. Say: Authenticity.

45
00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:37.523
Authenticity.

46
00:02:38.333 --> 00:02:42.193
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:48.396
Visal, you interviewed your grandmother about life in the 1970s. What did you learn?

48
00:02:49.483 --> 00:02:59.612
Details no book contains: how families hid rice, how songs were secretly remembered. An interview may reveal things missing from written records.

49
00:03:00.717 --> 00:03:03.192
But can memory be trusted after fifty years?

50
00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:14.787
Not completely. Some dates might be confused, and painful events may be described differently each time. So I compared her account with other sources.

51
00:03:15.883 --> 00:03:17.395
Was it difficult for her?

52
00:03:18.483 --> 00:03:26.987
At times. I learned to listen without interrupting, because a narrator might stay silent if she feels pressured.

53
00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:29.703
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:33.861
Grammar focus: Might and may for possibility.

55
00:03:34.161 --> 00:03:35.337
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:39.721
An interview may reveal details missing from written records.

57
00:03:40.617 --> 00:03:43.603
Some dates might be confused after fifty years.

58
00:03:44.517 --> 00:03:47.550
A narrator might stay silent if she feels pressured.

59
00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:59.482
'May' and 'might' express possibility, with 'might' slightly less certain. Use 'may/might + be + p.p.' for passive possibility, and 'may/might have + p.p.' for the past.

60
00:04:00.217 --> 00:04:04.158
Be careful! Don't say: An interview may reveals new details.

61
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.028
Say: An interview may reveal new details.

62
00:04:09.328 --> 00:04:11.258
After 'may', use the base verb.

63
00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:15.032
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:15.833 --> 00:04:17.380
Voices From the Past.

65
00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.385
Oral history records the spoken memories of people who lived through historical events.

66
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.417
It may preserve the experiences of groups who left few written documents, such as farmers, women and minorities.

67
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:36.579
Memories might be shaped by later events, emotions or retelling, so historians treat them critically.

68
00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:43.247
Nevertheless, a firsthand account may capture feelings and everyday details that official records ignore.

69
00:04:44.233 --> 00:04:51.179
In Cambodia, projects such as the Documentation Center have collected thousands of testimonies so that survivors' voices endure.

70
00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:56.419
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:57.283 --> 00:05:00.223
Her memory blank incomplete after so many years.

72
00:05:00.523 --> 00:05:04.235
A, might be. B, might is. C, might being.

73
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.892
The answer is A: might be.

74
00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.468
An interview blank reveal new details.

75
00:05:13.768 --> 00:05:16.933
A, may. B, may to. C, mays.

76
00:05:20.467 --> 00:05:22.431
The answer is A: may.

77
00:05:23.583 --> 00:05:26.848
A description from someone who experienced events is a blank.

78
00:05:27.148 --> 00:05:30.976
A, narrator. B, firsthand account. C, archive.

79
00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:36.835
The answer is B: firsthand account.

80
00:05:37.983 --> 00:05:40.749
The quality of being genuine and reliable is blank.

81
00:05:41.049 --> 00:05:45.039
A, memory. B, testimony. C, authenticity.

82
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.203
The answer is C: authenticity.

83
00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:56.230
According to the reading, oral history may preserve the experiences of…

84
00:05:56.530 --> 00:06:02.041
A, only officials. B, groups who left few written documents. C, historians.

85
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.399
The answer is B: groups who left few written documents.

86
00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:13.891
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:18.607
Why might an oral history interview reveal something a written record cannot?

88
00:06:35.417 --> 00:06:36.963
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:37.263 --> 00:07:15.067
An oral history interview might reveal emotions and everyday details that written records rarely include. Official documents usually record decisions and statistics, whereas a survivor may explain what those decisions felt like for an ordinary family. My grandmother, for example, described how people secretly shared food. Of course, memories may change over time and should be checked against other sources. Still, I believe oral history gives a voice to people whom official history often ignores.

90
00:07:15.367 --> 00:07:17.030
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:17.917 --> 00:07:22.276
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and might and may for possibility.

92
00:07:22.576 --> 00:07:27.701
Today's value is empathetic listening. Listen to understand, not only to reply.

93
00:07:28.001 --> 00:07:30.688
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:30.988 --> 00:07:34.650
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

